(defmodule main
  "bedit — a small Emacs-style GUI text editor, written in Brood on the editor
toolkit. This file is just the **entry point**: it opens a window (or takes the terminal)
and runs the `ui-run` loop under a supervisor, wiring together the layers that live in
their own files —

  - `startup`     — what an editor is prepared with: runtime, model, window
  - `lifeline`    — the loop under a supervisor: a dead loop auto-saves and restarts
  - `model`       — the `ui-run` model and the registries features plug into
  - `view`        — render a frame from the model
  - `input`       — fold a key or an event into the next model
  - `commands`    — the general editing commands, each a `model -> model`
  - `interactive` — the `defcommand` macro + M-x registry
  - `modes`       — modes as layers: keymaps + services
  - `remote`      — `--serve` / `--attach`, the daemon and its clients

and three std modules underneath: `buffer` (the rope-backed model), `display`
(render ops), and `ui` (`ui-run` over `(gui-display)`, a native window). Nothing
custom in any kernel — see CLAUDE.md.

Run it:

    nest run                 ; a *scratch* buffer
    nest run notes.txt       ; open notes.txt (created on C-x C-s if it doesn't exist)

Keys (Emacs-style; the full table lives in `input`): printable inserts; arrows or
C-f/C-b/C-n/C-p move, M-f/M-b by word, C-a/C-e / Home/End to line ends, M-</M-> to
buffer ends, C-v/M-v (or PageDown/PageUp) by a screenful; Enter
splits, Backspace/C-d/Delete remove; C-SPC sets the mark, C-w kills the region,
M-w copies, C-y yanks; C-/ (or C-x u) undoes, M-/ redoes; C-x C-f opens a file,
C-x b switches buffer, C-x ←/→ cycle, C-x k kills a buffer; C-x 2/3 split, C-x o
other pane, C-x 0/1 close panes, C-x l toggles line numbers (per pane), Ctrl+wheel
(or C-x C-= / C-x C-- / C-x C-0) zooms the pane under it a pixel at a time, that pane
only (M-x preset-select for a whole named window setup) / plain wheel scrolls; C-x C-e evals the form before point; C-x C-s
saves; C-x C-c / Esc / the window close button quits."
  (:use editor/ui) ; the ui-run loop
  (:use editor/frontend) ; gui-display
  (:use keyhelp) ; key / command labels, where-is, docstrings
  (:use clipboard)
  (:use model) ; ed-init
  (:use view) ; ed-view
  (:use input) ; ed-update
  (:use eval-commands) ; loaded with the image: C-x C-e and M-x eval-region / eval-buffer
  (:use offloop :only []) ; loaded with the image: the `:ready-wait` task-reply row a slow call waits in
  (:use shell-command) ; loaded with the image: M-! / M-| (C-u M-| must be prefix-consuming from the start)
  (:use gitbar) ; loaded with the image: git's row in `vcscore` (C-x g, C-x v, the mode line's branch)
  (:use mur) ; and mur's, with all of its porcelain — a backend whose row has not loaded is not offered
  ;; the idle-beat jobs and event handlers that must be live from the first event: each
  ;; module registers its rows when it loads (`model/register-idle-job`,
  ;; `model/register-event-handler`), so loading it here is all that wires it in — neither
  ;; the model nor the dispatcher names them
  (:use diagcheck) ; the advisory check (and its sandbox session client)
  (:use eldoc) ; the eldoc hint, the signature-due test, the completion preview
  (:use cape) ; loaded with the image: cape-file + cape-dabbrev completion sources
  (:use snippet :only []) ; loaded with the image: C-c &, the snippet keys as completions, TAB through fields
  (:use spell :only [spell-ranges]) ; loaded with the image: the idle-beat spell check (jinx), M-$ / C-M-$
  (:use pulse) ; a flash on the line after a jump (pulsar)
  ;; what paints and what takes the keys: a highlight source, a pane layer, a frame or key overlay
  ;; is a row, and an unloaded
  ;; row does nothing (`model/register-highlight-source`, `model/register-overlay`)
  (:use rainbow :only [])
  (:use glyphless :only [])
  (:use whitespace :only [])
  (:use fillindicator :only []) ; the fill-column rule
  (:use yankmedia :only []) ; M-x yank-media, and the handlers markdown and org name
  (:use isearch :only [])
  (:use query-replace :only [])
  (:use menu :only [])
  (:use headerline :only [])
  (:use avy :only [])
  (:use collabview :only []) ; other participants' selections and carets
  (:use ansicolor :only []) ; a program's colours on its output, under the mode's (a text-face row)
  (:use foldview :only []) ; a folded line's ellipsis
  (:use codefold :only []) ; the hideshow commands (C-c @) and code folds
  (:use searchedit :only []) ; editable search results (C-c C-p), project replace (C-x p %)
  (:use projectdiag :only []) ; the project's diagnostics (C-x p !)
  (:use systemtheme :only []) ; the window follows the desktop's light or dark setting
  (:use imageview :only []) ; an image opens as a picture (the :image presenter)
  (:use docview :only []) ; a PDF opens as its pages (the :document presenter)
  (:use help :only []) ; C-h k: its describe-key overlay
  (:use dired :only []) ; the :directory presenter
  (:use wdired :only []) ; C-x C-q in Dired: the names edited as text (its keymap names wdired)
  (:use hexl :only []) ; the :binary presenter
  (:use frames :only []) ; the :new-frame presenter, and its down handler
  (:use bugreport :only []) ; the error sink, and M-x report-bug
  (:use winner) ; C-c ← / C-c → window-layout undo (winner-mode)
  (:use tabbar :only []) ; C-x t: tabs, each a window layout (tab-bar-mode) — its row and keepers
  (:use dirlocals :only []) ; a project's .bedit-locals.blsp: loaded with the image, a visit reaches it by its row
  (:use solong) ; so-long: a file with a very long line opens truncated, M-x so-long-mode
  (:use saveplace :only [remember-places!]) ; loaded with the image: a kill and a visit reach it by its rows
  (:use reopen :only []) ; loaded with the image: a kill reaches it by its row, C-x x U reopens
  (:use slowlog) ; slow turns of the loop kept across sessions, M-x slow-turns
  (:use treewarm) ; a large tree-sitter buffer's parse, warmed off the loop
  (:use stringwarm) ; a large buffer's string cache, warmed off the loop
  (:use autosave) ; `#file#` sidecars
  (:use autorevert) ; files changed on disk reverted
  (:use sidecar) ; ed-recovery-note — the launch file's
  (:use recover) ; M-x recover-this-file / recover-session — the sidecars put back
  (:use filecoding) ; C-x RET f / r — a file's coding
  (:use sibling) ; M-x find-sibling-file
  (:use sudoedit) ; /sudo::PATH and M-x sudo-edit — a file edited as root, its mode-line chip
  (:use sshedit :only []) ; /ssh:host:PATH — a file on another machine, its mode-line chip
  (:use lifeline) ; the loop under a supervisor: a dead loop auto-saves and restarts
  (:use editor/buffer-registry :only [registry-entries]) ; a restarted loop finds its buffers
  (:use lsp-commands) ; LSP autostart / text push / signature, and the `:lsp-…` events
  (:use lsp-semantic :only [semantic-on?]) ; LSP semantic tokens: the idle ask, the carry, the paint layer
  (:use lsp-lens :only [lens-on?]) ; LSP code lenses: rows above their lines, M-x lsp-code-lens-run
  (:use projects) ; project-root — the current project shown on the model
  (:use config) ; init-config / window-mode — the user init file (~/.config/bedit/init.blsp)
  (:use startup) ; the startup path the window shares with --serve: runtime, model, window
  (:use editor/buffer) ; make-buffer
  (:use editor/buffer-file) ; buffer-from-file
  (:use editor/layers) ; set-buffer-type — the welcome scratch's :welcome stamp; the launch file's language
  ;; `:exclude [error]` — log's `error` would shadow the prelude's `error`.
  (:use log :exclude [error]) ; the async, safe logger — routed into *Messages* below
  (:use remote) ; native remote attach — the --serve/--attach daemon/emacsclient modes
  (:use supervisor :exclude [stop])) ; OTP-style supervision — keeps the logger alive (start-logging); drop its `stop` (gen made `stop` a bare prelude verb)
(def *welcome-template*
  ;; The keys are named as COMMANDS (`\\[…]`) rather than spelled out: this is the
  ;; first text a new user reads, and under the vim profile the Emacs spellings would be
  ;; false (there is not even an M-x). `keyhelp/ed-substitute-command-keys`
  ;; renders it against the live keymap at startup, so it follows a rebinding too.
  "hello — a tiny Emacs-style editor on the rope buffer framework.\n\nType to edit · \\[commands/cmd-find-file] open · \\[commands/cmd-switch-buffer] switch buffer · \\[editing/cmd-undo] undo · \\[eval-commands/cmd-eval-last-sexp] eval\n\\[region/cmd-set-mark] set mark · \\[killing/cmd-kill-region]/\\[killing/cmd-copy-region]/\\[killing/cmd-yank] kill/copy/yank · \\[lifecycle/cmd-save] save · \\[lifecycle/cmd-quit] quit\n\n➜ New to bedit? Click here: the interactive bedit tutorial · \\[tutor/cmd-tutorial]\n➜ Just the language? the Brood playground · \\[playground/cmd-brood-playground]\n")

(defn welcome-text (profile config)
  "The startup scratch's text with every key rendered for `profile`'s real bindings
(`keyhelp/ed-substitute-command-keys`). The editor always *starts* in the emacs
profile — there is no startup profile setting in `init.blsp` today — so `main`
passes `:emacs`; if one is added, pass it here and the text follows.

The template is `config`'s `:welcome` string when the init file sets one (the same
`\\[command]` key holes work there), else `*welcome-template*`. The link lines are
matched by substring (`modes/*welcome-links*`), so a custom template keeps the
clickable tutorial/playground links by keeping their marker text — or drops them."
  ;; The version footer is APPENDED rather than written into the template: the template is
  ;; static text with `\\[…]` key placeholders, and this line is neither static nor a key.
  ;; It costs one `project-release/build-info` at startup — a git call on a source run, none at all
  ;; in a release bundle, and only on the cold-start path that already reads the init file.
  (let (template (if (string? (:welcome config)) (:welcome config) *welcome-template*))
    ;; the profile with init.blsp's own :keys bound in: there is no model yet, but the
    ;; config is in hand, so the text names the keys the user will actually press
    (str (ed-substitute-command-keys (userkeys/profile-keymap-with profile
                                       (config/user-keys config))
           template)
      "\n"
      (about/version-line)
      "\n")))

(defn start-logging ()
  "Start the editor's logger under a one-for-one supervisor (parented to the editor
process), so killing the logger — e.g. with `d`/`x`/`k` in the *Process List* —
auto-restarts it (a `:permanent` child: a hard `(exit … :kill)` reaches the trapping
supervisor as a trappable `[:EXIT]`, which restarts it). The backend targets the
editor by its registered name (`:editor`), resolved *inside the supervisor at each
(re)start*, not a `(self)` captured here — so a restarted logger still streams its
lines to the live editor's `*Messages*`. Returns the supervisor's pid.

Prerequisite: `:editor` must already be registered (see `main`). Reused by tests."
  (start (list {:id :logger
                :start (fn () (start-logger {:level :info :backends (ed-log-backends)}))
                :restart :permanent})
    {:name :editor-sup :strategy :one-for-one}))

(defn- launch-buffer (file directory? project config)
  "`[buffer pending]`: the buffer the editor opens on — the file, or, for a scratch session,
behind a directory's dired listing, or while the file is `pending`, the welcome scratch.
That is typed :welcome: an ordinary editable buffer whose mode adds one thing, the
tutorial/playground lines are clickable links (modes.blsp); `config`'s :welcome replaces the
template text when set. `pending` is the file the window cannot start on, for
[[open-pending]]: `:ask` when it is large, `:read` when it is to be read off the loop, `{:bytes}`
for a binary, `{:error}` when it cannot be read; nil when there is none. The file is read
once."
  (let (welcome (editor/layers/set-buffer-type (make-buffer (welcome-text :emacs config)) :welcome))
    (cond
      (or (nil? file) directory?) [welcome nil]
      ;; large: asked about once there is a window to ask in ([[open-pending]])
      (pool/ed-large-file-size file (config/setting config :large-file-warning-threshold))
        [welcome :ask]
      ;; big enough to read off the loop: the window opens at once, the file landing in it
      (pool/large-read? file) [welcome :read]
      else
      ;; read ONCE: a binary file's bytes, or why it could not be read, go to
      ;; [[open-pending]] rather than failing the launch
        (let (contents (if (file/exists? file)
                         (try (pool/ed-read-file file) (catch e {:error e}))
                         {:text nil}))
          (if (contains? contents :text)
            (let (opened (assoc (charset/with-coding (if (nil? (:text contents))
                                                       (find-file-buffer file)
                                                       (buffer-from-file file (:text contents)))
                                  (:coding contents))
                           :name (file-display-name file project))
                  ;; save-place: a file named on the command line opens where you left it too
                  placed (if (config/setting config :save-place)
                           (saveplace/restore-place opened)
                           opened))
              ;; so-long, as every open does; `start-loop` says so (`solong/so-long-note`)
              [(first (solong/so-long-visit placed (config/setting config :so-long-threshold))) nil])
            [welcome contents])))))

(defn- open-pending (m file pending)
  "The launch `file` the window could not start on, opened now there is a model: `:ask` —
too large to read without asking (`pool/ed-visit-file`); `:read` — large enough to read off the
loop, `Loading…` until it lands (`pool/ed-find-file-soon`); `{:bytes}` — a binary, as a hex
view; `{:error}` — why it could not be read, said. nil: nothing pending."
  ;; nothing pending when there is no file (a scratch session): `path/absolute` of nil is a
  ;; contract error under `nest run`, so the path is made only when there is one to open
  (cond
    (nil? pending) m
    (= pending :ask) (pool/ed-visit-file m (path/absolute file))
    (= pending :read) (pool/ed-find-file-soon m (path/absolute file))
    ;; an image or a PDF to its viewer, any other binary to the hex view — as find-file
    ;; routes them
    (:bytes pending)
      (model/ed-present m
        (pool/binary-role file)
        (message m "not text, and no hex view is loaded")
        (path/absolute file)
        (:project-root m)
        (:bytes pending))
    else (message m (str "open failed: " (error-message (:error pending))))))

(defn- live-model (buf project display config frontend)
  "The model the live editor starts from: `startup/prepare-model` (init.blsp's model-level
settings and the live flags a served session shares — recentf, save-place) over the flags
only a LOCAL editor has, which pure-model tests (via `ed-init`) omit. The buffer conventions
are set before `ed-init` stamps the launch buffer (`startup/prepare-buffer-settings`).
`frontend` is `:window` or `:terminal` ([[run-window]])."
  (startup/prepare-buffer-settings config)
  (startup/prepare-model
    (merge
      (assoc (ed-init buf)
        :project-root project
        ;; mirror kills/copies to the OS clipboard and yank from it (clipboard/ed-clipboard-write
        ;; + ed-clipboard-read). On only for the live editor — pure model tests build via
        ;; `ed-init` without it, so they never touch the process-global OS clipboard (which
        ;; would make them order-dependent); a served session has its CLIENT's instead. In
        ;; the terminal it is the one [[run-loop]] binds: the desktop's, or the terminal's
        :os-clipboard true
        ;; the kill ring every frame shares (clipboard/ed-start-kill-agent) — a frame inherits the
        ;; pid, so a kill in one window is yankable in all of them
        :kill-agent (ed-start-kill-agent)
        ;; the desktop's reduced-motion preference, which `:reduce-motion :auto` follows
        :os-reduce-motion (startup/desktop-reduce-motion config))
      (if (= frontend :terminal)
        ;; the terminal (`bedit -nw`, or no display): no `:gui`, so the view draws its
        ;; cell-quantised fallbacks (a plain `rect`, not a sub-cell `frect`), and no window
        ;; for what asks one — and what only a window can do (a second frame) says so
        {:terminal true}
        ;; the native GUI frontend: the view may use sub-cell `frect` rendering — rounded
        ;; floating cards, etc.; the window id, for what asks the window itself (M-x latency)
        {:gui true :window (:window display)}))
    config))

(defn- start-editor-logging ()
  "Route the logger into the editor: a `process-backend` to the editor (ui-run) process
sends each formatted line as `[:io/write s]`, which `ed-update` folds into *Messages*. Async +
safe — the editor never blocks on a log, and the one logger process serialises writes.
Registers the editor pid as `:editor` (so any process can `(send (whereis :editor) [:io/write
\"…\"])`) *before* `start-logging`, which supervises the logger and addresses the editor by
that name — so a killed logger restarts and the fresh one still streams here. (The init.blsp
warnings come later, from `startup/load-packages-later`, once every setting is declared.)"
  (proc/register :editor (self))
  (start-logging)
  (log/info "bedit started"))

(defn- appearance-of (config)
  "The type setup the window was given (`startup/window-font`, plus the config's line
height), for `appearance/apply!`. The window has no getter for any of it, so this is what
lets Ctrl+wheel step from the real size and `M-x preset-save` name what you are actually
looking at (src/appearance.blsp) instead of a guess."
  {:family (:family (startup/window-font config))
   :height (:height (startup/window-font config))
   :line-height (config/setting config :line-height)})

(defn- open-window (config frontend)
  "The native window (a {:enter :leave :size :draw :poll :window} map), set up from `config`.
`:app-id` is how the desktop identifies it as *this* app — without it the window matches no
installed .desktop entry and GNOME paints its generic fallback icon (config-paths/*app-id*,
assets/README.md). `:title` is only the pre-first-event placeholder; `input` sets the real
one (`ed-window-title`) from then on. `config/*window-options*` carries `:key-repeat :os`: a
held key repeats at the desktop's own delay and rate (brood ADR-441, input.blsp's key-repeat
section), and `config/window-options` adds the init file's `:option-meta` — which macOS
Option key is Meta, the other composing (brood ADR-541). Opened by the loop process, which polls it: a window's input goes to the process
that opened it.

Returns `[display warnings]`: the window, and what its setup has to say — the init file's `:font`
not installed (`startup/configure-window`), its `:theme` not existing — for [[run-loop]] to log
into *Messages* once the logger runs (a warning logged here, before it, went to stderr) and
for the first loop's echo area.

In the TERMINAL (`frontend` `:terminal`) it is brood's terminal display
(`editor/frontend/*term-display*`, brood ADR-586) and only the colours are set up: a terminal's
font is the terminal's."
  (if (= frontend :terminal)
    (let (theme-warning (startup/apply-look config false))
      (do
        ;; the terminal's default face follows the palette, as a window's background does
        (theme/use-terminal-look!)
        [editor/frontend/*term-display* (seq/reject [theme-warning] nil?)]))
    (let (display (gui-display (assoc (config/window-options config) :title *app-name*))
          font-warning (startup/configure-window config display)
          ;; the colours over the window's own background; init.blsp's `:theme` names one
          ;; that may not exist
          theme-warning (startup/apply-look config true))
      [display (seq/reject [font-warning theme-warning] nil?)])))

(defn- run-loop (supervisor m display warnings)
  "Run `ui-run` on `m` in `display`, this process being the editor: the logger routed here,
`warnings` (the window setup's, [[open-window]]) logged into *Messages* through it,
blocking calls reported (brood ADR-517), and `supervisor` (`lifeline/supervise`) told the
window and buffer registry, so it can save and restart if this loop dies. THE FLIP
(docs/actor-architecture.md): every pool buffer in the live window is backed by its own
process — `hosted/hosted-reconcile` at the loop tail hosts each slot as it appears — and
named in the buffer registry, so a second frame (C-x 5 2), or this loop's successor, links
its slots to the same processes (docs/frames-plan.md). Tests/headless models never set
`:host-buffers?`, so the pure suite stays pure."
  (do
    (start-editor-logging)
    (doseq (warning warnings) (log/warn warning))
    ;; the loop must keep answering: a native call that blocks it is reported (brood ADR-517)
    (slowlog/responsive!)
    (lifeline/announce! supervisor (:window display) (:buffer-reg m) (frames/frames-live-of m))
    (if (:terminal m)
      ;; a terminal's kills reach the desktop's clipboard when there is one, else the
      ;; terminal's (OSC 52) — over ssh, the machine the user sits at
      (let (clipboard (startup/terminal-clipboard-bindings (editor/frontend/display-available?)))
        ;; and the runtime has no window of its own: what reaches for one (a theme's font,
        ;; `theme/gui-only`) is skipped rather than starting a GUI thread with no display
        (binding (*windowless-runtime* true
                  *clipboard-writer* (:writer clipboard)
                  *primary-writer* (:primary-writer clipboard)
                  *clipboard-reader* (:reader clipboard)
                  *primary-reader* (:primary-reader clipboard))
          (ui-run (assoc m :on-hangup (fn (mm sig) (display-hangup mm sig supervisor)))
            ed-view ed-update display)))
      ;; a window whose compositor or X server went away is told `[:hangup :display-lost]`
      ;; (brood ADR-631): the same save, then the loop ends
      (ui-run (assoc m :on-hangup (fn (mm sig) (display-hangup mm sig supervisor)))
        ed-view ed-update display))))

(defn hangup-note (sig saved)
  "The line a hang-up leaves in the log: how the display went (`sig`: `:hup`, `:term`, or a
window's `:display-lost`) and how many unsaved files `saved` names."
  (str "bedit: "
    (case sig :hup "hung up" :display-lost "lost its display" "terminated")
    "; auto-saved "
    (count saved)
    " unsaved file(s)"))

(defn- display-hangup (m sig supervisor)
  "What a session does when its display goes before the loop ends (brood ADR-586's
`[:hangup sig]`): SIGHUP or SIGTERM under a terminal, the terminal then restored, or a window's
compositor or X server gone (`:display-lost`, brood ADR-631). Emacs's — every buffer with
unsaved text is auto-saved to its sidecar (`lifeline/emergency-save!`, reading the buffer
processes, a file edited as root to its private one), so `M-x recover-session` finds it next
time — and the `supervisor` told not to restart the loop however it ends: its display is gone."
  (lifeline/no-restart! supervisor)
  (log/warn (hangup-note sig (lifeline/emergency-save! (:buffer-reg m)))))

(defn- open-frontend (config frontend)
  "`[display warnings frontend note]`: [[open-window]] for `frontend`, and the frontend it
became. A window that does not open — a forwarded X display that refuses it, a compositor
gone — falls back to the terminal when stdin and stdout are one, `note` saying why
(`startup/window-failed-note`); without a terminal to fall back to, the error stands."
  (if (= frontend :terminal)
    (let ([display warnings] (open-window config :terminal)) [display warnings :terminal nil])
    (let (opened (try (open-window config :window) (catch e {:failed e})))
      (cond
        (vector? opened) [(first opened) (second opened) :window nil]
        (startup/terminal-unusable (os/stdin-tty?) (os/stdout-tty?)) (throw (:failed opened))
        else
          (let ([display warnings] (open-window config :terminal))
            [display
              warnings
              :terminal (startup/window-failed-note (error-message (:failed opened)))])))))

(defn- start-loop (supervisor init launch buffer pending)
  "The first editor loop: open the window (or take the terminal) on the launch `buffer` (a
directory argument — `bedit .` — shows its dired listing over the scratch buffer; a launch
file that is large, binary or unreadable is `pending`, opened once there is a model —
[[open-pending]]), then [[run-loop]]. `init` is the init file as read for this start
(`startup/read-init`); `launch` the [[run-window]] facts: `:file`, `:directory?`,
`:project`, `:frontend`, the `:frontend-note` to say and what a `:clean` start leaves out
(`startup/clean-start`)."
  (let (config (:config init)
        file (:file launch)
        directory? (:directory? launch)
        project (:project launch)
        [display warnings frontend window-note] (open-frontend config (:frontend launch))
        started (live-model buffer project display config frontend)
        ;; dired auto-loads on the qualified call
        listed (if directory? (dired/ed-show-dired started (path/absolute file)) started)
        recovery (when (and file (not directory?) (nil? pending))
                   (ed-recovery-note (path/absolute file)))
        ;; an init file that does not read leaves every setting at its default, and a font
        ;; that is not installed leaves the bundled one: said in the echo area at once, not
        ;; only among the *Messages* lines
        note (string/join (seq/reject (append [(:frontend-note launch)
                                                window-note
                                                (startup/clean-start-note (:clean launch))
                                                (:note init)]
                                        warnings
                                        [recovery (solong/so-long-note buffer)])
                            nil?)
               " · ")
        ;; a large file is read off the loop although the loop has not started yet
        ;; (`pool/reading-off-loop`)
        m (pool/reading-off-loop
            (fn ()
              (let (opened (assoc (open-pending (if (empty? note) listed (message listed note))
                                    file pending)
                             ;; the project this session belongs to, whatever is visited later
                             :session-root (:project-root listed)))
                ;; after the note, so a question it asks (a large file) is what the echo area
                ;; shows; no file named: the project's last session, its files and layout,
                ;; comes back
                (if (nil? file) (lastsession/restore-for-start opened) opened)))))
    (do
      ;; discovered on launch → known for C-x p p (once: the project the model settled on,
      ;; which a directory's dired may have resolved afresh)
      (remember-project! (:project-root m))
      (startup/load-packages-later config startup/say-in-log (:clean launch))
      ;; a bedit that died on a signal last time left a record (brood ADR-635): kept for
      ;; M-x report-bug and said, once the window is up
      (spawn (fn ()
               (receive (after 1500 nil))
               (doseq (line (bugreport/adopt-crashes!)) (startup/say-in-log :summary line))))
      (run-loop supervisor
        (frames/frames-registry-start (appearance/apply! (assoc m :host-buffers? true)
                                        (appearance-of config)))
        display
        warnings))))

(defn- restart-loop (supervisor config state)
  "A loop started after the last one died (`lifeline/supervise`): a fresh window over every
buffer in the registry — their processes outlived the loop, so the text is all there —
showing the first file buffer, the live flags the dead loop announced carried over, and the
echo area saying what happened. Its layout, minibuffer and transient state are gone."
  (let (registry (:registry state)
        entries (registry-entries registry)
        file-entry (seq/find (if (vector? entries) entries []) (fn (e) (:file (or (:meta e) {}))))
        show (if file-entry (:name file-entry) "*scratch*")
        ;; the dead loop's frontend: a terminal session restarts in the terminal
        [display warnings] (open-window config (if (:terminal (:live state)) :terminal :window))
        seeded (frames/frames-seed-model registry show (:live state))
        m (config/apply-to-model (assoc (dissoc seeded :frame?) :window (:window display)) config))
    (run-loop supervisor
      (message (appearance/apply! m (appearance-of config))
        "the editor's loop died and was restarted — every buffer is here; check *Messages*; M-x report-bug sends what killed it")
      display
      warnings)))

(defn- report-death (death)
  "Say on stderr and in the log that the editor's loop died, what was auto-saved, and
whether it is being restarted — the one record a crash leaves outside the window."
  (let (line (str (if (:signal death)
                    (str "bedit: told to end (" (:signal death))
                    (str "bedit: the editor loop died (" (:reason death)))
               "); "
               (lifeline/saved-note (:saved death))
               (if (:restarting? death) " — restarting" " — not restarting")))
    (do
      (io/puts line :to (io/stderr-port))
      (log/warn line)
      ;; a loop that died of an error is kept for M-x report-bug, as a caught one is
      (when (and (nil? (:signal death)) (:reason death)) (ed-report-error (:reason death) :loop))
      nil)))

(defn- run-window (file frontend frontend-note clean)
  "Open the local window on `file` (nil for a scratch session) — or, `frontend` `:terminal`,
take the terminal, saying `frontend-note` in the echo area — and run the editor loop under
`lifeline/supervise`: a loop that dies has its unsaved buffers auto-saved and is restarted
over the same buffers. Returns when the editor quits."
  (let (init (startup/read-init-for clean) ; ~/.config/bedit/init.blsp, unless -q / -Q
        config (:config init)
        ;; `bedit .` / `bedit some/dir` — open dired, not a text file
        directory? (and file (file/dir? file))
        project (startup/launch-project file directory?)
        ;; the runtime's half of startup, shared with `--serve`: the user's grammars
        ;; declared, the slow-turn log, minibuffer history (one user here, so it may be on)
        _runtime (startup/prepare-runtime config true)
        ;; after the grammars: a file of a parsed language with a line-lexer stand-in is typed by
        ;; whether its grammar is ready (`model/ed-grammar-fallback`), so it must be declared first
        [buffer pending] (launch-buffer file directory? project config)
        outcome (lifeline/supervise (fn (supervisor)
                                      (start-loop supervisor
                                        init
                                        {:file file
                                         :directory? directory?
                                         :project project
                                         :frontend frontend
                                         :frontend-note frontend-note
                                         :clean clean}
                                        buffer
                                        pending))
                  (fn (supervisor state) (restart-loop supervisor config state))
                  report-death))
    (cond
      (= outcome :died) 1
      (vector? outcome) (lifeline/signal-exit-code (second outcome))
      else nil)))

(defn main (& args)
  "Open the editor in a native window. With a `file` argument (`nest run <path>`)
it edits that file — reading it if it exists, else starting an unsaved buffer backed
by it so C-x C-s creates it (vim/emacs `find-file`); with none, a *scratch* buffer.
Applies the init file's `:font` / `:font-size` as the window's base cell font. Ctrl+mouse-wheel (and C-x C-= / C-x C--
/ C-x C-0) zooms ONE PANE a pixel at a time — the pane under the pointer, and only it: it is
painted in a `cell-region` at its own size and the window's font never moves
(`appearance/zoom-step`, brood ADR-363); `M-x preset-select` switches whole named window
setups — what a screen share wants.

`-nw` / `--no-window` runs the editor in the TERMINAL instead (Emacs's `-nw`), over brood's
terminal display (`editor/frontend/*term-display*`, ADR-586): changed cells drawn, the mouse and a
bracketed paste read, kills on the desktop's clipboard or — over ssh — the terminal's (OSC
52). With no display to open a window on (no `$DISPLAY` / `$WAYLAND_DISPLAY`, an ssh
session) it runs there by itself, and says so. A cell has one size there, so a zoom is
refused, and there is one frame.

With `--serve` (`bedit --name ed --serve [file]`) this runs as a daemon that also opens the
host's own window (add `--headless` for a windowless background daemon; `--shared` — alias
`--collab` — for the collaborative session: one document, everyone their own named caret,
`M-x follow` to ride someone's), and `--attach NAME` (`bedit --attach ed`) opens a window attached to a
daemon — the emacsclient model (`src/remote.blsp`). Both are handled up front; otherwise the
normal local window runs, its setup unchanged. `main` is variadic because the standalone `bedit` passes all
CLI tokens through positionally (`bedit --name ed --serve --shared` → 4 args); the file is
the first non-flag token, and remote mode is detected from `(argv)`.

`-Q` starts clean, as Emacs's: no init file and no installed packages (`-q` /
`--no-init-file` skips only the init file, `--no-packages` only the packages), said in the
echo area — how a bug is told from a setting or a package (`startup/clean-start`).

`--version` / `-V` prints the one-line version stamp (`src/about.blsp`) and exits without
opening a display — as does the runtime's own reserved `--brood-build-info` on a release
bundle, which prints the same facts plus the build id and features."
  (cond
    ;; `--version` answers and exits without touching the display — see
    ;; `about/version-arg?` for why that ordering is the point, not a detail.
    (about/version-arg? args) (do (io/puts (about/version-line)) 0)
    (remote/remote-main? args) (remote/remote-main args)
    else (run-local args)))

(defn- run-local (args)
  "The local editor for the command line `args`: in a window, or in the terminal when `-nw`
/ `--no-window` asks or there is no display (`startup/choose-frontend`) — refused, on
stderr, when the terminal is not one either (stdin or stdout redirected)."
  (let ([frontend note] (startup/choose-frontend args (editor/frontend/display-available?))
        unusable (when (= frontend :terminal)
                   (startup/terminal-unusable (os/stdin-tty?) (os/stdout-tty?))))
    (if unusable
      (do (io/puts unusable :to (io/stderr-port)) 1)
      (run-window (remote/remote-positional args) frontend note (startup/clean-start args)))))
